.TH "PERFORMANCECONTAINERTIMING.FIRSTRENDERTIME" "3JS" "October 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformanceContainerTiming.firstRenderTime \-
PerformanceContainerTiming: firstRenderTime property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]firstRenderTime\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] interface returns the time at
which the rendering update ended for the first paint that contributed
content to this container.
.PP
This remains fixed at the time of the container\(cqs very first paint
after registration, across every entry reported for that container.
This differs from \f[CR]paintTime\f[R] and \f[CR]startTime\f[R], which
reflect the paint that generated a particular entry.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]DOMHighResTimeStamp\f[R] measured relative to the time origin.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Observing that firstRenderTime stays fixed
This example demonstrates that \f[CR]firstRenderTime\f[R] stays fixed at
the container\(cqs very first paint (while \f[CR]startTime\f[R] and
\f[CR]paintTime\f[R] change with every entry).
.SS HTML
First define a \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element that is marked as a
container root identified as \f[CR]\(dqhero\(dq\f[R] by its
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute, along with a button to reset the
example.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqreset\(dq>Reset</\f[B]button\f[R]>
<\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq>
  <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Hero content</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
Note that there is also hidden HTML (and code) for displaying log
information.
New log entries are added at the top of the log.
.SS JavaScript
The following code first checks whether the browser supports
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R] entries: if not, it logs that the feature
is not supported.
It then creates a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R] that logs each
entry\(cqs \f[CR]lastPaintedElement\f[R], and its
\f[CR]firstRenderTime\f[R], \f[CR]paintTime\f[R], and
\f[CR]startTime\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] container = document.querySelector(\(dqsection\(dq);

\f[B]if\f[R] (PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes.includes(\(dqcontainer\(dq)) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] entry \f[B]of\f[R] list.getEntries()) {
      log(
        \(galastPaintedElement: ${entry.lastPaintedElement?.outerHTML ?? \(dqnull\(dq}\(ga +
          \(ga firstRenderTime: ${entry.firstRenderTime.toFixed(1)}\(ga +
          \(ga startTime: ${entry.startTime.toFixed(1)}\(ga +
          \(ga paintTime: ${entry.paintTime.toFixed(1)}\(ga,
      );
    }
  });
  observer.observe({ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(dqThis feature is not supported by your browser.\(dq);
}
.EE
.PP
We then use \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to add a new paragraph to the
container every second, stopping after five paragraphs.
Each paragraph triggers a new paint and timing entry.
Note that we add content on a timer rather than when the user clicks a
button, because no entries are reported after the user interacts with
the page (see Reporting stops after scrolling or user input).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] maxParagraphs = 5;
\f[B]let\f[R] count = 0;

\f[B]const\f[R] timer = setInterval(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  count++;
  \f[B]const\f[R] paragraph = document.createElement(\(dqp\(dq);
  paragraph.textContent = \(gaNew paragraph ${count}\(ga;
  container.appendChild(paragraph);
  \f[B]if\f[R] (count >= maxParagraphs) {
    clearInterval(timer);
  }
}, 1000);
.EE
.PP
Last of all we add a click event handler to reset the example by
reloading the page.
.IP
.EX
document.querySelector(\(dq#reset\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  window.location.reload();
});
.EE
.SS Result
A new paragraph is added every second.
Each paragraph should trigger a new log where the
\f[CR]firstRenderTime\f[R] stays the same but the other properties
change value.
Click the \f[B]Reset\f[R] button to restart the example.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming.paintTime\f[R]
